{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target argues that preferring a theory because it has 'withstood the most severe tests so far' is a pragmatic bet on the 'currently fittest survivor,' analogous to biological selection. However, this defense fails on Popper's own terms because the utility of such a 'bet' presupposes that past fitness (corroboration) is a reliable indicator of future performance (problem-solving capacity). In biological evolution, we assume the environment remains sufficiently stable for past adaptations to confer present advantage; similarly, in science, acting on a corroborated theory only makes pragmatic sense if one assumes the future will resemble the past regarding the theory's domain. By asserting that the theory is the 'most robust instrument... so far' and therefore the rational choice for action, the target implicitly assigns a higher subjective probability to its future success compared to a falsified or less-tested theory. This reinstates the inductive principle—that the unobserved future will resemble the observed past—which Popper explicitly rejected. The distinction between 'acting on' and 'believing in' collapses here: if there is no rational basis to expect the 'fittest' theory to continue working better than an arbitrary alternative, the preference is not defensible as rational pragmatism but is merely an irrational habit, which contradicts the target's claim of a 'defensible... rule of action.'","premise":"The target's reliance on 'Evolutionary Epistemology' to justify preferring better-corroborated theories smuggles in the very inductive assumption Popper sought to eliminate.","successor_question":"If past corroboration provides no inductive reason to expect future success, on what non-arbitrary grounds can a scientist claim it is 'pragmatic' to choose Theory A over Theory B, rather than flipping a coin?","target":"beabdaebcfb2e0a9329c87340d71cd6dc6b6de31a1a7f5c64a3b9fe85cb38311"}]}